# Merge behaviour for the files that two concurrent rounds would both touch.
#
# Why this file exists: exploration can be parallelised across git worktrees
# (`tools/worktree.sh`), but round *closure* cannot — several tracked files are
# written once per round and would collide. Two of those collisions have
# opposite correct answers, and git cannot guess either one, so they are
# declared here rather than discovered during a merge.

# The Phase B declaration is append-only: one row per round, rows independent
# (`docs/phase-b-rounds.tsv`, see CLAUDE.md "Working contract"). Two rounds each
# adding their own row is not a conflict in any meaningful sense — union keeps
# both, which is exactly the intended result.
#
# Deliberately NOT applied to the other `docs/*.tsv` budgets
# (reference-names-budget, containment-budget, pointer-reach-budget, ...): those
# are *rewritten wholesale* by their tool's `--write-budget`, so a union merge
# would resurrect stale rows and silently inflate a ratchet. Append-only is a
# property of this one file, not of the extension.
docs/phase-b-rounds.tsv merge=union

# The atomic store is the spec SSOT and must NEVER be hand-edited — CLAUDE.md
# says so, and every mutation goes through the Mnemosyne typed primitives.
#
# That rule and a textual auto-merge are incompatible: resolving a conflict in
# this file IS a hand edit, and a *successful* auto-merge is worse than a failed
# one, because it produces a store nobody authored and nobody read. `-merge`
# makes git refuse to combine the two sides — it keeps ours and declares the
# conflict — which forces the only correct procedure: drop one side's ledger
# writes, let the other land, then re-apply through `mnemosyne-cli`.
#
# So this line does not make parallel rounds work. It makes the fact that they
# do not work arrive as a refusal instead of as a corrupted 14 MB JSON.
docs/.atomic/workspace.atomic.json -merge
